# Requirement coverage — <project or capability>

**One authority per fact.** `REQUIREMENTS.md` (verbatim transcription of the
official specification, one stable ID per requirement — `R1`, `R2`, …) is the
sole authority for *which* requirements exist. This file,
`REQUIREMENT_COVERAGE.md`, is a **derived view** of how each one is being met.
Every requirement holds its own row here, independently — no grouping by
capability, task, or commit may erase an individual requirement's row.

Checked by `validate_requirement_coverage.py`, which compares this file
against `REQUIREMENTS.md` **by identifier, in both directions** — not against
a count either file declares about itself. A requirement present in
`REQUIREMENTS.md` with no row here fails the check; a row here naming a
requirement `REQUIREMENTS.md` does not declare also fails it.

| ID | Source | Capability | Task | Implementation status | Required validation | Evidence | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|

`Status` is tri-state-aware: `PASSED` / `N/A` / `N/A-with-reason` /
`HITL_ACCEPTED_LIMITATION` / `BLOCKED`, or left open while work continues. A
row whose status is not one of those and carries no evidence fails the check —
that combination means nobody decided (test-or-stated-gap rule,
`skills/core/requirement-interpretation.md`).

## Validation

    python3 templates/validate_requirement_coverage.py REQUIREMENTS.md REQUIREMENT_COVERAGE.md

Application points: capability closure (coordinator runs it before `CLOSED` is
recorded) and project close (coordinator runs it before the human acceptance
packet).
